now the Conservatives might have  outperformed expectations at the local  elections but if the results were  replicated at a general election they'd  be unable to form a majority so is there  really much to celebrate well let's ask  the party's chairman Brandon Lewis who  joins us now so you lost seats lost  councils are you really happy with the  results well I've said actually since  Thursday it was a good night for us in  the sense we gained councils we  outperform what people expected but it's  very much stage one and there is a yeah  we get what we gained we're certainly  excused but I think we will have gained  at the end about seven councils we would  have taken control either from Labour or  from no overall control and we managed  to hold on to councils in London that  Labour said they would turn London  completely we even technically gained  barn it back with the majority of 13 but  I've been very clear about this there's  no complacency we've got a big piece of  work to do it this was just stage one of  what will be a four-year piece of work  it's the real risk here that you're kind  of piling on votes in your comfort areas  so older voters white voters voters in  suburbs and the Shires where labour is  doing the same with kind of metropolitan  voters in cities it feels as if it's  kind of a deadlock that's a stasis that  they've got two losers would you agree  with that assessment no actually I think  two points and if you like at two levels  I think first we'll around the country  over the last few months when I've been  traveling around to all parts of the  country I've seen both of our volunteers  activist candidates and the people on  the doorsteps of all backgrounds all  ages in all parts of the country  supporting what we're doing and actually  getting involved that's a good thing and  actually at a secondary level if that  was the case we wouldn't have held on to  the council's labour expected to win  like Wandsworth Westminster and indeed I  don't put it down to luck at all I put  it down to the really hard work of good  local volunteers good counsellors and  candidates who've been out there talking  about the issues that matter to local  people every day low council tax  particularly ones within Westminster and  good frontline services are you worried  that although you've taken votes off you  kept away from the cities now the real  challenge is taking votes off labour and  if you look at these results that's not  really much evidence that you have been  successful in doing that well again in  London we gained seats from Labour in  Hillingdon and in Barnet of course  we've got a majority of 13 now but I'm  very open about the fact we as I say  we've got work to do over the next four  years this is just stage one in terms of  that level of work and we've got to make  sure that we are talking to people as I  said from all backgrounds exactly what  the prime minister said delivering for  everybody right across the United  Kingdom now earlier in the show we paid  a film from ones with where I went to  YouTube talk to voters and to find out  know what they were feeling about the  main parties and there was one young man  who really stood out to me with what he  said and I'll just read you what he did  say he said as a young man if I put  myself online as a conservative I just  get shouted down I can't say openly I'm  a conservative because if I do people  will just say oh that guys are  conservative bring him down I mean do  you think he's effectively saying that  young people are ashamed to say that  they're conservative  actually I recognised the the challenge  that he's got but I see a very different  way actually what I've seen online not  just for people who once say they  support the Conservative Party but  actually we've seen it with moderate  Labour MPs the abuse that we see online  and the sheer attrition from the hard  left which I think there is an argument  is actually purposely to try and put  people off having proper robust debate  it's why we were very clear about having  a respect pledge we need to have a  robust debate but it's got to be respect  and what we do see online is this really  kind of nasty sometimes really quite  personal actually in some cases even  calling for physical abuse and attacks  it's completely unacceptable we've got  to make sure all of us in public life  prepared to take that on get involved in  the debate both online and in the  general public to create that air space  and more people can feel confident about  coming forward and talking about what  they care about but that I want to go  back to that point as well though  because if people do feel confident to  come out and say I'm a conservative is  that because they're worried about the  image of the party and how it's seen  it's not helped is it for example when  conservative councillors post racist or  Rio repost or racist jokes on social  media and of course I'm talking about a  joke that was made by all posted again  by Rosemarie Carol from pendel Council  and I can't even refer to what she said  because frankly it's pre abhorrent I  mean that is not helping your cause is  it well I what was posted the actual  it was called a joke I don't see as a  joke it's completely unacceptable and  let's be very clear about this because a  lot of misinformation being put out  there the moment I think it's right to  get a chance to just clear that up not  just for her but actually more generally  this was a situation that occurred last  year actually in about June last year  her position has been from the very  beginning that she accidentally posted  something she wanted to delete she was  suspended by the party at that point for  three months the labor run counts which  was of course supported by the BNP at  the time put a punishment in place she's  also gone through diversity training  since then and has been a Conservative  councillor for some time so there's a  lot of misinformation not a lot of facts  out there well it's not can come back  let's not have back into the concept I  think gonna be very clear we don't have  double standards first of all she's been  through training she's apologized she  has had punishment now this was all  dealt with locally at the time I am  going to be having a look at this but  there's also a big contrast between  where we are and where labour are first  of all of course the leader of the  opposition's position is he didn't  understand the mural this is a  councillor who has gone through  punishment and training over here but we  also when we have these issues we deal  with them and she's a good example that  that was dealt with at the time it was  dealt with locally to be fair as well  are you happy with how it's been dealt  because I just want to go back to is  that it's hard because she can't  actually refer to what was written but  it really is a pauran it's repugnant are  you happy with someone like that  representing the Conservative Party who  even if it was an accident  posted on there well let's be very clear  her position is it was an accident she  was suspended she's been through  diversity training the punishment she  had was endorsed by the Labour Party and  the BNP who supported him on that  council at the time he's been very clear  about where the facts are but it is  something I'm going to be having a look  at because we've been very clear from  the beginning in our party if we have an  issue come out as we have done over the  last few months when it comes out we  won't do a Labour Party does and pretend  it doesn't exist we will deal with it so  you're gonna look at it again does that  mean she could be kicked out of the  party I'm not going to prejudge any kind  of due diligence work he's right that we  go through this properly but also again  want to stress this was dealt with  locally last year and the position that  was taken was one that was actually in  not just endorsed by it but actually 